    {
      "id": "arg-variadic-max",
      "doc": "A variadic stops at its bound, so the words past it belong to whatever comes next — and with nothing after it, the third word has nowhere to go. `var_too_many` cannot arise for a variadic any more: it describes a repeatable flag given more times than its bound allows. This used to be recorded as a divergence — the corpus wanted `var_too_many` and usage-lib said `unexpected_arg` — and adopting the limit reading is what made the two agree.",
      "spec": "name \"ex\"\nbin \"ex\"\narg \"<files>\" var=#true var_max=2\n",
      "argv": [
        "a",
        "b",
        "c"
      ],
      "expect": {
        "error": "unexpected_arg"
      },
      "spec_json": {
        "name": "ex",
        "bin": "ex",
        "cmd": {
